<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="subsec-u5-quadratic-forms-definiteness">
  <title>Quadratic forms and definiteness</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      So far, a matrix <m>A</m> has defined the <xref ref="def-3-2-matrix-transformation" text="custom">matrix map</xref>
      <me>
        \mathbf{x}\longmapsto A\mathbf{x},
      </me>
      whose output is a vector. The same matrix also defines the scalar-valued rule
      <me>
        q_A(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{x}^TA\mathbf{x}.
      </me>
      This rule is quadratic rather than linear. Although the input now appears twice, eigenvectors and the <xref ref="thm-principal-axes" text="custom">orthogonal diagonalization theorem</xref> still make the rule simple.
    </p>
  </introduction>
		<definition xml:id="def-u5-quadratic-form">
			<statement>
				<p>
					The <term>quadratic form</term> associated with an <m>n \times n</m> matrix <m>A</m> is the scalar-valued function <m>q_A</m> on <m>\R^n</m> defined by <m>q_A(\mathbf{x}) = \mathbf{x}^T A \mathbf{x}</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</definition>

  <activity xml:id="act-u5-linear-quadratic-scaling">
    <title>Linear and quadratic scaling</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>A</m> be an <m>n\times n</m> matrix, let <m>\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb R^n</m>, and let <m>t\in\mathbb R</m>.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><p>Compare <m>A(t\mathbf{x})</m> with <m>tA\mathbf{x}</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p>Compare <m>q_A(t\mathbf{x})</m> with <m>tq_A(\mathbf{x})</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p>Which rule is linear in <m>\mathbf{x}</m>? Which rule is quadratic?</p></li>
      </ol>
      <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U5-LO4, U1-LO7 | C+R | Core]</c></p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Matrix multiplication is linear, so
        <me>
          A(t\mathbf{x})=tA\mathbf{x}.
        </me>
        For the quadratic form,
        <md>
          <mrow>q_A(t\mathbf{x})</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp=(t\mathbf{x})^TA(t\mathbf{x})</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp=t^2\mathbf{x}^TA\mathbf{x}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp=t^2q_A(\mathbf{x}).</mrow>
        </md>
        Thus <m>\mathbf{x}\mapsto A\mathbf{x}</m> is linear, while <m>\mathbf{x}\mapsto q_A(\mathbf{x})</m> is quadratic.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </activity>

  <p>
    The remaining results use an orthogonal eigenvector basis to remove cross terms and expose the sign of the quadratic form.
  </p>

  <p>
    The next definition records the eigenvalue sign patterns that will control the sign of the quadratic form.
  </p>

  <definition xml:id="thm-7-2-diagonalization">
    <title>Positive definite, negative definite, and indefinite matrices</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        A symmetric matrix is called <term>positive definite</term> if all of its eigenvalues are strictly positive. It is called <term>negative definite</term> if all of its eigenvalues are strictly negative. It is called <term>indefinite</term> if it has at least one strictly positive eigenvalue and at least one strictly negative eigenvalue.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

		<activity xml:id="act-u5-quadratic-form-as-curvature">
			<title>A quadratic form as curvature</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Let
					<me>
						H=
						\begin{bmatrix}
						4\amp0\\
						0\amp1
						\end{bmatrix}.
					</me>
				</p>
				<ol marker="a">
					<li>
						<p>
							Compute <m>\mathbf{h}^T H\mathbf{h}</m> for
							<me>
								\mathbf{h}=
								\begin{bmatrix}
								1\\
								0
								\end{bmatrix},
								\qquad
								\mathbf{h}=
								\begin{bmatrix}
								0\\
								1
								\end{bmatrix},
								\qquad
								\mathbf{h}=
								\begin{bmatrix}
								1\\
								1
								\end{bmatrix}.
							</me>
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Write <m>\mathbf{h}^T H\mathbf{h}</m> for a general vector
							<me>
								\mathbf{h}=
								\begin{bmatrix}
								h_1\\
								h_2
								\end{bmatrix}.
							</me>
						</p>
					</li>
					<li><p>In which coordinate direction is the quadratic form larger?</p></li>
				</ol>
				<p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U5-LO4, U5-LO5 | P+C | Core]</c></p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					We compute
					<me>
						\begin{bmatrix}
						1\\
						0
						\end{bmatrix}^T
						H
						\begin{bmatrix}
						1\\
						0
						\end{bmatrix}
						=4,
					</me>
					<me>
						\begin{bmatrix}
						0\\
						1
						\end{bmatrix}^T
						H
						\begin{bmatrix}
						0\\
						1
						\end{bmatrix}
						=1,
					</me>
					and
					<me>
						\begin{bmatrix}
						1\\
						1
						\end{bmatrix}^T
						H
						\begin{bmatrix}
						1\\
						1
						\end{bmatrix}
						=5.
					</me>
					For a general vector,
					<me>
						\mathbf{h}^T H\mathbf{h}=4h_1^2+h_2^2.
					</me>
					The quadratic form is larger in the first coordinate direction than in the second coordinate direction. For Hessians, this kind of comparison measures curvature in different directions.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</activity>

		<p>
			If we can understand quadratic forms, we can understand the quadratic approximations of functions at critical points.
		</p>

		<activity>
			<statement>
				<p>
					For a symmetric <m>3 \times 3</m> matrix <m>A = [A_{ij}]</m>, write out the quadratic form <m>q_A(\mathbf{x}) = \mathbf{x}^T A \mathbf{x}</m> explicitly in terms of the entries of <m>A</m> and the components of <m>\mathbf{x} = (x_1, x_2, x_3)</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>

			<solution>
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>
							q_A(\mathbf{x}) \amp = \begin{bmatrix} x_1 \amp x_2 \amp x_3 \end{bmatrix}
							\begin{bmatrix}
							A_{11} \amp A_{12} \amp A_{13} \\
							A_{21} \amp A_{22} \amp A_{23} \\
							A_{31} \amp A_{32} \amp A_{33}
							\end{bmatrix}
							\begin{bmatrix} x_1 \\ x_2 \\ x_3 \end{bmatrix}
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = \begin{bmatrix} x_1 \amp x_2 \amp x_3 \end{bmatrix}
							\begin{bmatrix}
							A_{11}x_1 + A_{12}x_2 + A_{13}x_3 \\
							A_{21}x_1 + A_{22}x_2 + A_{23}x_3 \\
							A_{31}x_1 + A_{32}x_2 + A_{33}x_3
							\end{bmatrix}
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = x_1(A_{11}x_1 + A_{12}x_2 + A_{13}x_3)
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp + x_2(A_{21}x_1 + A_{22}x_2 + A_{23}x_3)
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp + x_3(A_{31}x_1 + A_{32}x_2 + A_{33}x_3)
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = A_{11}x_1^2 + A_{22}x_2^2 + A_{33}x_3^2
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp + A_{12}x_1x_2 + A_{13}x_1x_3 + A_{21}x_2x_1
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp + A_{23}x_2x_3 + A_{31}x_3x_1 + A_{32}x_3x_2
						</mrow>
					</md>
					Since <m>A</m> is symmetric, we have <m>A_{ij} = A_{ji}</m> for all <m>i, j</m>. Using this property:
					<md>
						<mrow>
							\amp q_A(\mathbf{x}) = A_{11}x_1^2 + A_{22}x_2^2 + A_{33}x_3^2 + (A_{12} + A_{21})x_1x_2
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp + (A_{13} + A_{31})x_1x_3 + (A_{23} + A_{32})x_2x_3
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = A_{11}x_1^2 + A_{22}x_2^2 + A_{33}x_3^2
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp + 2A_{12}x_1x_2 + 2A_{13}x_1x_3 + 2A_{23}x_2x_3
						</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</activity>

		<remark>
			<p>
				More generally, for a symmetric <m>n \times n</m> matrix <m>A = [A_{ij}]</m>, the quadratic form <m>q_A(\mathbf{x}) = \mathbf{x}^T A \mathbf{x}</m> can be written as:
				<me>
					q_A(\mathbf{x}) = \mathbf{x}^T A \mathbf{x}
					= \sum_{i=1}^n \sum_{j=1}^n A_{ij} x_i x_j
					= \sum_{i=1}^n A_{ii}x_i^2 + 2\sum_{1 \leq i \lt j \leq n} A_{ij}x_i x_j
				</me>
				In particular, note that if <m>A</m> is a diagonal matrix, then
				<me>
					q_A(\mathbf{x}) = A_{11} x_1^2 + \cdots + A_{nn} x_n^2
				</me>
				which is simple to understand. We will use spectral theory to show that a rotation of a coordinate system reduces a quadratic form to a quadratic form defined by a diagonal matrix. The signs of the diagonal entries will then tell us the behavior of the critical point.
			</p>
		</remark>

		<p>
			Recall the <xref ref="thm-principal-axes" text="custom">orthogonal diagonalization theorem</xref>, which says that there is an orthogonal matrix <m>Q</m> so that <m>Q^T A Q</m> is a diagonal matrix <m>D</m>.
		</p>

		<theorem xml:id="thm-10-1-diagonalization-theorem">
			<title>Diagonalization theorem</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Consider a quadratic form <m>q_A</m>, defined in terms of a symmetric matrix <m>A</m>. Suppose <m>Q</m> is an orthogonal matrix such that
					<me>
						Q^TAQ=D=\operatorname{diag}(\lambda_1,\ldots,\lambda_n).
					</me>
					If we set <m>\mathbf{x}=Q\mathbf{y}</m>, then
					<me>
						q_A(\mathbf{x})=\lambda_1y_1^2+\cdots+\lambda_ny_n^2.
					</me>
				</p>
			</statement>

			<proof>
				<p>
					Since <m>\mathbf{x}=Q\mathbf{y}</m>, we have
					<md>
						<mrow>
							q_A(\mathbf{x})
							\amp = \mathbf{x}^TA\mathbf{x}
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = (Q\mathbf{y})^TA(Q\mathbf{y})
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = \mathbf{y}^TQ^TAQ\mathbf{y}
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = \mathbf{y}^TD\mathbf{y}
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = \lambda_1y_1^2+\cdots+\lambda_ny_n^2.
						</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
			</proof>
		</theorem>

		<definition>
			<title>The principal axes</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					If <m>A</m> is a symmetric, <m>n \times n</m> matrix, then an orthonormal basis of eigenvectors of <m>A</m> is called a set of <term>principal axes</term> for the quadratic form <m>q_A</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</definition>

		<activity>
			<introduction>
				<p>
					Consider the quadratic form <m>q(x,y) = 13x^2 - 6xy + 5y^2</m>.
				</p>
			</introduction>

			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Find the symmetric matrix <m>A</m> such that <m>q = q_A</m>, i.e., such that
						<me>
							q(x,y) = \begin{bmatrix} x \amp y \end{bmatrix} A \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix}
						</me>.
					</p>
				</statement>

				<solution>
					<p>
						If
						<me>
							A = \begin{bmatrix} a \amp b \\ b \amp c \end{bmatrix}
						</me>,
						then
						<me>
							q_A(x,y)=ax^2+2bxy+cy^2.
						</me>
						So <m>a = 13</m>, <m>b = -3</m>, and <m>c = 5</m>, i.e.,
						<me>
							A = \begin{bmatrix} 13 \amp -3 \\ -3 \amp 5 \end{bmatrix}
						</me>.
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Find the eigenvalues and a basis of eigenvectors for <m>A</m>.
					</p>
				</statement>

				<solution>
					<p>
						The <em>characteristic polynomial</em> of <m>A</m> (recall <xref ref="def-7-1-char-polynomial"/>) is given by
						<md>
							<mrow>
								c_A(\lambda) \amp = \det(\lambda I_2 - A)
							</mrow>
							<mrow>
								\amp = \det \begin{bmatrix} \lambda - 13 \amp 3 \\ 3 \amp \lambda - 5 \end{bmatrix}
							</mrow>
							<mrow>
								\amp = (\lambda - 13) (\lambda - 5) - 9
							</mrow>
							<mrow>
								\amp = \lambda^2 - 18 \lambda + 56
							</mrow>
							<mrow>
								\amp = (\lambda - 14)(\lambda - 4)
							</mrow>
						</md>.
						So <m>\lambda = 4</m> and <m>\lambda = 14</m> are the two eigenvalues of <m>A</m>.
					</p>

					<p>
						For the eigenvalue <m>\lambda_1 = 14</m>, we solve <m>(A - \lambda_1 I)\mathbf{v}_1 = \mathbf{0}</m>:
						<me>
							\begin{bmatrix} -1 \amp -3 \\ -3 \amp -9 \end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix} v_1 \\ v_2 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}
						</me>
						That is, <m>v_1 = -3v_2</m>. Taking <m>v_2 = 1</m>, we get <m>v_1 = -3</m>. So an eigenvector for <m>\lambda_1</m> is
						<me>\mathbf{v}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} -3 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</me>.
						For the eigenvalue <m>\lambda_2 = 4</m>, we solve <m>(A - \lambda_2 I)\mathbf{v}_2 = \mathbf{0}</m>:
						<me>
							\begin{bmatrix} 9 \amp -3 \\ -3 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix} v_1 \\ v_2 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}
						</me>
						That is, <m>v_2 = 3v_1</m>. Taking <m>v_1 = 1</m>, we get <m>v_2 = 3</m>. So an eigenvector for <m>\lambda_2</m> is
						<me>\mathbf{v}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix}</me>.
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Apply the previous theorem to express <m>q(x_1, x_2)</m> in terms of new coordinates <m>y_1</m> and <m>y_2</m> that eliminate the cross term.
					</p>
				</statement>

				<solution>
					<p>
						We first normalize the eigenvectors to get:
						<md>
							<mrow>
								\mathbf{v}'_1 \amp = \frac{\mathbf{v}_1}{||\mathbf{v}_1||} = \frac{\begin{bmatrix} -3 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}}{\sqrt{(-3)^2 + 1^2}} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{10}}\begin{bmatrix} -3 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
							</mrow>
							<mrow>
								\mathbf{v}'_2 \amp = \frac{\mathbf{v}_2}{||\mathbf{v}_2||} = \frac{\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix}}{\sqrt{1^2 + 3^2}} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{10}}\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix}
							</mrow>
						</md>

						The orthogonal matrix <m>Q</m> with these unit eigenvectors as columns diagonalizes <m>A</m>. When we express a vector <m>\mathbf{x} = Q\mathbf{y} = y_1\mathbf{v}'_1 + y_2\mathbf{v}'_2</m>, the quadratic form becomes:
						<md>
							<mrow>
								q(\mathbf{x}) = \mathbf{x}^T A \mathbf{x} \amp = \mathbf{y}^T Q^T A Q \mathbf{y}
							</mrow>
							<mrow>
								\amp = \mathbf{y}^T
								\begin{bmatrix}
								\lambda_1 \amp 0 \\
								0 \amp \lambda_2
								\end{bmatrix}
								\mathbf{y}
							</mrow>
							<mrow>
								\amp = \lambda_1 y_1^2 + \lambda_2 y_2^2
							</mrow>
							<mrow>
								\amp = 14 y_1^2 + 4 y_2^2.
							</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>
		</activity>

		<figure xml:id="fig-u5-positive-definite-level-curves">
			<caption>
				Level curves of <m>q(x,y)=13x^2-6xy+5y^2</m>. The principal-axis directions <m>\mathbf{v}'_1</m> and <m>\mathbf{v}'_2</m> are orthonormal eigenvectors of <m>A</m> with eigenvalues <m>14</m> and <m>4</m>, respectively.
			</caption>
			<image source="figures/unit5/u5-positive-definite-level-curves" width="72%">
				<shortdescription>Nested elliptical level curves of a positive-definite quadratic form, together with its two orthogonal principal axes.</shortdescription>
				<description>
					<p>
						Four nested ellipses centered at the origin show the level sets <m>q(x,y)=c</m> for <m>c=5,10,20,30</m>. The first principal axis <m>\mathbf{v}'_1=(-3,1)/\sqrt{10}</m> runs from upper left to lower right, and the second principal axis <m>\mathbf{v}'_2=(1,3)/\sqrt{10}</m> runs from lower left to upper right. Each ellipse is shorter in the <m>\mathbf{v}'_1</m> direction, which has eigenvalue <m>14</m>, and longer in the <m>\mathbf{v}'_2</m> direction, which has eigenvalue <m>4</m>.
					</p>
				</description>
			</image>
		</figure>

		<activity>
			<introduction>
				<p>
					Consider the quadratic form
					<me>
						q(x,y) = x^2 + 4xy - 2y^2
					</me>.
				</p>
			</introduction>

			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Find the symmetric matrix <m>A</m> such that <m>q = q_A</m>.
					</p>
				</statement>

				<solution>
					<p>
						The matrix is
						<me>
							A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \\ 2 \amp -2 \end{bmatrix}
						</me>.
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Find the eigenvalues and corresponding eigenvectors of <m>A</m>.
					</p>
				</statement>

				<solution>
					<p>
						The characteristic polynomial is
						<md>
							<mrow>
								\det(A-\lambda I)
								\amp =
								\det
								\begin{bmatrix}
								1-\lambda \amp 2\\
								2 \amp -2-\lambda
								\end{bmatrix}
							</mrow>
							<mrow>
								\amp =
								(1-\lambda)(-2-\lambda)-4
							</mrow>
							<mrow>
								\amp =
								\lambda^2+\lambda-6
							</mrow>
							<mrow>
								\amp =
								(\lambda-2)(\lambda+3).
							</mrow>
						</md>
						Thus the eigenvalues are <m>2</m> and <m>-3</m>.
						For eigenvalue <m>\lambda_1 = 2</m>, we solve <m>(A - \lambda_1 I)\mathbf{v}_1 = \mathbf{0}</m>:
						<me>
							\begin{bmatrix} -1 \amp 2 \\ 2 \amp -4 \end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix} v_1 \\ v_2 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}
						</me>

						That is, <m>v_1 = 2v_2</m>. Taking <m>v_2 = 1</m>, we get <m>v_1 = 2</m>. So
						<me>
							\mathbf{v}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
						</me>
						is an eigenvector for <m>\lambda_1</m>. For the eigenvalue <m>\lambda_2 = -3</m>, we solve <m>(A - \lambda_2 I)\mathbf{v}_2 = \mathbf{0}</m>:
						<me>
							\begin{bmatrix} 4 \amp 2 \\ 2 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix} v_1 \\ v_2 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}
						</me>

						That is, <m>v_2 = -2v_1</m>. Taking <m>v_1 = 1</m>, we get <m>v_2 = -2</m>. So an eigenvector for <m>\lambda_2</m> is <m>\mathbf{v}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -2 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Apply the previous theorem to express <m>q(x_1, x_2)</m> in terms of new coordinates <m>y_1</m> and <m>y_2</m> that eliminate the cross term, and classify the level curves <m>q(x_1, x_2) = c</m> for different values of <m>c</m>.
					</p>
				</statement>

				<solution>
					<p>
						We first normalize the eigenvectors to get:
						<md>
							<mrow>
								\mathbf{v}'_1 \amp = \frac{\mathbf{v}_1}{||\mathbf{v}_1||} = \frac{\begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}}{\sqrt{2^2 + 1^2}} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{5}}\begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
							</mrow>
							<mrow>
								\mathbf{v}'_2 \amp = \frac{\mathbf{v}_2}{||\mathbf{v}_2||} = \frac{\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -2 \end{bmatrix}}{\sqrt{1^2 + (-2)^2}} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{5}}\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -2 \end{bmatrix}
							</mrow>
						</md>
						The orthogonal matrix <m>Q</m> with these unit eigenvectors as columns diagonalizes <m>A</m>. When we express a vector <m>\mathbf{x} = Q\mathbf{y} = y_1\mathbf{v}'_1 + y_2\mathbf{v}'_2</m>, the quadratic form becomes:
						<md>
							<mrow>
								q(\mathbf{x}) = \mathbf{x}^T A \mathbf{x} \amp = \mathbf{y}^T Q^T A Q \mathbf{y}
							</mrow>
							<mrow>
								\amp = \mathbf{y}^T
								\begin{bmatrix}
								\lambda_1 \amp 0 \\
								0 \amp \lambda_2
								\end{bmatrix}
								\mathbf{y}
							</mrow>
							<mrow>
								\amp = \lambda_1 y_1^2 + \lambda_2 y_2^2
							</mrow>
							<mrow>
								\amp = 2 y_1^2 - 3 y_2^2
							</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>
		</activity>

		<figure xml:id="fig-u5-indefinite-level-curves">
			<caption>
				Level curves of <m>q(x,y)=x^2+4xy-2y^2</m>. In principal-axis coordinates, <m>q=2y_1^2-3y_2^2</m>.
			</caption>
			<image source="figures/unit5/u5-indefinite-level-curves" width="72%">
				<shortdescription>Hyperbolic level curves of an indefinite quadratic form, together with its principal axes and zero-level asymptotes.</shortdescription>
				<description>
					<p>
						The blue branches are the level set <m>q(x,y)=5</m> and open along <m>\mathbf{v}'_1=(2,1)/\sqrt{5}</m>, the eigenvector with positive eigenvalue <m>2</m>. The red branches are the level set <m>q(x,y)=-4</m> and open along <m>\mathbf{v}'_2=(1,-2)/\sqrt{5}</m>, the eigenvector with negative eigenvalue <m>-3</m>. Two dashed lines show the zero level set <m>q(x,y)=0</m>; these lines are asymptotes for the hyperbolas.
					</p>
				</description>
			</image>
		</figure>

		<theorem xml:id="thm-10-1-positive-negative-definite-inequlaily">
			<statement>
				<p>
					A symmetric matrix <m>A</m> is positive definite if and only if <m>\mathbf{x}^T A \mathbf{x} > 0</m> for every vector <m>\mathbf{x} \neq \mathbf{0}</m> in <m>\R^n</m>. It is negative definite if and only if <m>\mathbf{x}^T A \mathbf{x} \lt 0</m> for every vector <m>\mathbf{x} \neq \mathbf{0}</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>

			<proof>
				<p>
					By <xref ref="thm-10-1-diagonalization-theorem"/>, there is an orthogonal matrix <m>Q</m> such that
					<me>Q^TAQ=D=\operatorname{diag}(\lambda_1,\ldots,\lambda_n)</me>,
					where <m>\lambda_1,\ldots,\lambda_n</m> are the eigenvalues of <m>A</m>. Set <m>\mathbf{y}=Q^T\mathbf{x}</m>, so <m>\mathbf{x}=Q\mathbf{y}</m>. Because <m>Q</m> is invertible, <m>\mathbf{x}\neq\mathbf{0}</m> if and only if <m>\mathbf{y}\neq\mathbf{0}</m>. The diagonalization theorem gives
					<me>
						\mathbf{x}^TA\mathbf{x}
						=
						\lambda_1y_1^2+\cdots+\lambda_ny_n^2.
					</me>
				</p>
				<p>
					Suppose first that <m>A</m> is positive definite. Then every <m>\lambda_i</m> is strictly positive. If <m>\mathbf{x}\neq\mathbf{0}</m>, then <m>\mathbf{y}\neq\mathbf{0}</m>, so at least one coordinate <m>y_i</m> is nonzero. Therefore <m>\lambda_1y_1^2+\cdots+\lambda_ny_n^2>0</m>, and hence <m>\mathbf{x}^TA\mathbf{x}>0</m>.
				</p>
				<p>
					Conversely, suppose <m>\mathbf{x}^TA\mathbf{x}>0</m> for every <m>\mathbf{x}\neq\mathbf{0}</m>. For each <m>i</m>, take <m>\mathbf{y}=\mathbf{e}_i</m> and <m>\mathbf{x}=Q\mathbf{e}_i</m>. Then <m>\mathbf{x}\neq\mathbf{0}</m> and
					<me>\mathbf{x}^TA\mathbf{x}=\lambda_i>0</me>.
					Thus every eigenvalue of <m>A</m> is strictly positive, so <m>A</m> is positive definite.
				</p>
				<p>
					The negative-definite case is analogous. If every <m>\lambda_i\lt 0</m>, then <m>\lambda_1y_1^2+\cdots+\lambda_ny_n^2\lt 0</m> for every <m>\mathbf{y}\neq\mathbf{0}</m>, so <m>\mathbf{x}^TA\mathbf{x}\lt 0</m> for every <m>\mathbf{x}\neq\mathbf{0}</m>. Conversely, if this quadratic form is strictly negative for every nonzero <m>\mathbf{x}</m>, taking <m>\mathbf{x}=Q\mathbf{e}_i</m> gives <m>\lambda_i\lt 0</m> for each <m>i</m>. Hence <m>A</m> is negative definite.
				</p>
			</proof>
		</theorem>

  <note xml:id="bridge-hessian-quadratic-approximation">
    <title>Second-order optimization</title>
    <p>
      At a critical point, the linear part of the local approximation disappears. The next term is quadratic:
      <me>f(\mathbf{a}+\mathbf{h})\approx f(\mathbf{a})+\frac12\mathbf{h}^T H_f(\mathbf{a})\mathbf{h}.</me>
      Thus the <xref ref="sec-second-derivative-test-and-saddle-points" text="custom">second derivative test</xref> is a linear algebra test applied to the Hessian matrix.
    </p>
  </note>
</section>
